1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,380
On the 29th of September, 1918,

2
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:06,420
the two men who ran Germany's
war went to see their emperor.

3
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:12,380
Field Marshal Paul von Hindenburg and
General Erich Ludendorff told Kaiser Wilhelm

4
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:15,940
the Second that they could no
longer guarantee the Western Front.

5
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:17,060
Not for weeks.

6
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:18,140
Not for days.

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:19,260
For two hours.

8
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:22,480
Germany needed an armistice,
and it needed one immediately.

9
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:25,620
The Kaiser could hardly believe
it, and you can see why.

10
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:30,880
Six months earlier, the same army had
made the deepest advance either side had

11
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,360
managed on the Western Front since 1914.

12
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:38,820
In early June, its leading troops had been
about fifty-six kilometres from Paris.

13
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:44,520
So how does an army go from the outskirts
of Paris to two hours from collapse in a

14
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:45,280
single summer?

15
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:49,600
Or had the war really been lost long
before that summer even started?

16
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:52,180
Ask around and you'll get a list.

17
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:57,780
The Marne, in 1914, which one historian has
called the most important land battle of

18
00:00:57,780 --> 00:00:58,800
the twentieth century.

19
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:02,220
The British blockade that
slowly starved Germany.

20
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:04,260
The bloodbath at Verdun.

21
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:06,900
A great sea battle off
the coast of Denmark.

22
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:11,440
A decision taken at a castle
in Silesia in January 1917.

23
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:14,020
A black day in August 1918.

24
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:19,080
And for twenty years after the war, a
lot of Germans gave a different answer

25
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:19,540
altogether.

26
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:23,460
They said their army had never
been beaten in the field at all.

27
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:26,960
So, what was the real turning
point of World War One?

28
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:28,840
Sir, it ended in 1918.

29
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:30,940
Isn't that the turning point?

30
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:32,860
That's when the train arrived, Porter.

31
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:35,420
I want to know when it left the rails.

32
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:38,840
Eight moments, two
alliances, one hourglass.

33
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:40,400
Here's why an hourglass.

34
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:43,760
Every big German plan in this
war came with a deadline.

35
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:47,340
In 1914, beat France
before Russia was ready.

36
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:51,260
In 1917, starve Britain
before America could arrive.

37
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:55,880
In 1918, win in the west before
the Americans arrived in strength.

38
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:58,560
Germany was always racing a clock.

39
00:01:58,960 --> 00:01:59,900
So here's the rule.

40
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:02,480
A turning point isn't
the day the guns stopped.

41
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:06,340
It's the day Germany ran out of
ways to win, whatever it did next.

42
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:10,140
We'll go in order, look at each
moment from both sides of the wire,

43
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:11,180
and stamp it.

44
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:13,120
Turned, or not yet.

45
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:16,620
And one of these moments does
something none of the others do.

46
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:19,200
It turns the hourglass back over.

47
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:20,440
Glass is full.

48
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:22,660
Timetable says forty days to Paris.

49
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:23,360
Let's see.

50
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:27,220
Start with the problem Germany had
before a single shot was fired.

51
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:30,120
It sat in the middle of
Europe between two allies,

52
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:33,000
France in the west and Russia in the east.

53
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:35,720
A war with one meant a war with both.

54
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:39,880
So the German General Staff built
its whole war around a timetable.

55
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:45,580
Defeat France fast, in about six weeks,
then put the army on trains and turn east

56
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:48,160
before the huge, slow
Russian army was ready.

57
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:50,820
The road to Paris ran through Belgium.

58
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:54,860
The plan was first drawn up by
Field Marshal Alfred von Schlieffen,

59
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:58,760
then reworked by his
successor, Helmuth von Moltke.

60
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:03,480
It sent the German right wing in a great
wheel through neutral Belgium and northern

61
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:03,960
France.

62
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:07,100
The wheel would sweep
round to the west of Paris,

63
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:10,760
then trap and destroy the
French armies east of the city.

64
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:16,240
It was a plan that depended on speed, on
railways and on everything happening on

65
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:16,640
time.

66
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:20,300
And in August 1914, for a while, it did.

67
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:22,880
The German armies poured through Belgium.

68
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:25,960
The French had planned for
a German attack in the east,

69
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:30,600
in Alsace and Lorraine, and their
own offensives there failed badly.

70
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:34,780
Their commander, General Joseph
Joffre, clung to that plan,

71
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:38,660
even as one of his army
commanders, Charles Lanrezac,

72
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:42,440
kept warning him that the real German
blow was coming through Belgium.

73
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:48,340
On the 24th of August, Joffre finally
admitted that his left flank was in danger.

74
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:51,900
By then, the French and British
armies were in full retreat.

75
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:53,500
It was a hot summer.

76
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:57,480
Hundreds of thousands of men
marched south in wool uniforms,

77
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:00,300
carrying packs of around
twenty-seven kilograms.

78
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:03,720
The Germans kept going
because they believed Paris,

79
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:06,500
and the end of the war,
were only days away.

80
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:09,360
But the German wheel was
already losing weight.

81
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:13,240
Worried about the Russians, and
perhaps a little overconfident,

82
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:18,100
Moltke pulled two whole army corps
out of the west and sent them east.

83
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:22,760
Two more were left behind to besiege
fortresses at Antwerp and Maubeuge.

84
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:27,900
Altogether, the German armies in France
were down by two hundred thousand men or

85
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:28,180
more.

86
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:32,620
Moltke realised he no longer had
the strength to swing west of Paris,

87
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:37,740
so he changed the plan and ordered his
right wing to pass east of the city instead.

88
00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:42,760
The commander of that right wing, General
Alexander von Kluck and his First Army,

89
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:47,140
read his orders broadly, or ignored
them, depending on who you ask.

90
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:51,300
He turned southeast, chasing what
he thought was a beaten enemy.

91
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:55,080
On the 3rd of September his
troops crossed the river Marne,

92
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:55,900
east of Paris.

93
00:04:55,900 --> 00:05:00,620
Kluck didn't know that a new French army
had been assembled in and around the

94
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:02,200
capital, on his flank.

95
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:06,340
And he had written off the British,
who seemed to have left the battle.

96
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:10,740
The military governor of Paris, an
old general named Joseph Gallieni,

97
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:11,840
saw the opening.

98
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:16,120
Joffre, who for all his
mistakes stayed remarkably calm,

99
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:21,100
and by most accounts still insisted on
a good dinner and eight hours' sleep,

100
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:24,180
ordered the counterattack
on the 4th of September.

101
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:28,220
He had been shifting troops by rail
from his eastern armies to the Marne.

102
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:34,140
When the battle began, about nine hundred
and eighty thousand French and a hundred

103
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:38,440
thousand British soldiers faced about
seven hundred and fifty thousand Germans.

104
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:43,620
For a week, the armies fought along a front
some two hundred and thirty kilometres

105
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:44,120
long.

106
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:50,080
By the 9th of September, the two German
armies on the right were in danger of being

107
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:54,000
encircled, and they were ordered
to fall back to the river Aisne.

108
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:56,800
The estimated casualties were enormous.

109
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:02,420
Around two hundred and fifty thousand French,
nearly three hundred thousand Germans,

110
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:04,460
and almost thirteen thousand British.

111
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:06,620
Moltke's health broke down.

112
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:09,300
On the 14th of September, he was replaced.

113
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:13,920
And there's a famous line he's supposed
to have said to the Kaiser after the

114
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:14,260
retreat.

115
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:16,740
Your Majesty, we have lost the war.

116
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:18,240
Sir, so he said it.

117
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:19,400
September 1914.

118
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:20,540
Did he?

119
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:22,080
Check the paperwork, Porter.

120
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:23,420
Here's the paperwork.

121
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:25,480
Nobody can show that he said it.

122
00:06:25,860 --> 00:06:29,600
Even the historians who repeat the
line admit we simply don't know.

123
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:32,160
Keep the sentence in your pocket anyway.

124
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:36,460
We'll find out later whether it was
true, even if it was never said.

125
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:39,020
What we do know is what happened next.

126
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:42,220
Both sides tried to go around
each other to the north,

127
00:06:42,220 --> 00:06:44,740
in what became known
as the race to the sea.

128
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:48,280
By late October, the race had
reached the Belgian coast.

129
00:06:48,980 --> 00:06:50,900
There was nowhere left to go around.

130
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:54,580
From the North Sea to the Swiss
border, the armies dug in.

131
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:56,140
The trenches had arrived.

132
00:06:56,680 --> 00:06:58,420
So, did the sand run out here?

133
00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:01,100
Germany's fast way to
win was gone for good.

134
00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:04,360
There would be no six-week
war, no quick turn east.

135
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:09,560
Germany was now stuck in exactly the long,
two-front war its whole plan was built to

136
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:09,920
avoid.

137
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:14,540
But it still held almost all of Belgium
and a large slice of northern France,

138
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:16,440
and nobody had beaten its army.

139
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:18,140
The short war was dead.

140
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:19,340
The war was not.

141
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:20,760
Small glass, empty.

142
00:07:21,140 --> 00:07:22,360
I'm turning over the big one.

143
00:07:22,620 --> 00:07:23,080
Not yet.

144
00:07:23,620 --> 00:07:26,600
And the big glass had a crack
in it from the very beginning.

145
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:30,420
Germany fed its people and
its factories with imports.

146
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:35,280
Before the war, more than half of what it
imported was raw material for industry:

147
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:38,700
cotton and wool, hides,
copper and other metals,

148
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,460
oil, rubber, and saltpetre for explosives.

149
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:44,040
Much of its food came from abroad too.

150
00:07:44,300 --> 00:07:47,300
And most of the world's
sea lanes ran past Britain.

151
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:51,980
The British had decided how they would
use that geography before the war even

152
00:07:51,980 --> 00:07:52,400
started.

153
00:07:53,020 --> 00:07:54,200
By July 1914,

154
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,120
they had settled on what they
called a distant blockade.

155
00:07:58,540 --> 00:08:02,240
The Royal Navy didn't need to
fight its way into German harbours.

156
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:03,980
It simply closed the doors.

157
00:08:03,980 --> 00:08:09,860
Ships patrolled the narrow English Channel,
and the Northern Patrol shut the gap of

158
00:08:09,860 --> 00:08:13,400
about two hundred and fifty kilometres
between Shetland and Norway,

159
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:16,740
with the Grand Fleet waiting
behind at Scapa Flow.

160
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:23,340
On the 2nd of November, 1914, Britain
declared the whole North Sea a military area.

161
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:28,820
From March 1915, it would seize any cargo
it believed was heading for Germany.

162
00:08:29,060 --> 00:08:30,640
It leaked, at first.

163
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:33,320
Neutral neighbours like
the Netherlands, Denmark,

164
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:39,240
Norway and Sweden suddenly started importing
a lot more from America and selling a

165
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:40,160
lot more to Germany.

166
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:46,480
But by 1915, Germany's imports had still
fallen by more than half from their pre-war

167
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:46,920
level.

168
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:48,880
Then came a bad harvest.

169
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:55,040
In the winter of 1916 and 1917, much of
the potato crop rotted in the fields,

170
00:08:55,440 --> 00:08:57,320
and Germans got through it on turnips.

171
00:08:57,740 --> 00:08:59,460
They still call it the Turnip Winter.

172
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:05,640
By 1917, official rations were down to
around a thousand calories a day in winter,

173
00:09:06,260 --> 00:09:10,160
a third of what Germany's own health
office said a working man needed.

174
00:09:10,620 --> 00:09:15,704
In December 1918, German officials
claimed that the blockade had killed seven

175
00:09:15,704 --> 00:09:19,880
hundred and sixty-three thousand
civilians through hunger and disease.

176
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:25,020
A study ten years later put it at
four hundred and twenty-four thousand,

177
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:30,820
and later historians are more cautious
still, because the flu pandemic of 1918 gets

178
00:09:30,820 --> 00:09:31,960
mixed up in the numbers.

179
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:33,780
Sir, that's not a moment.

180
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:35,740
That's every day for four years.

181
00:09:36,380 --> 00:09:37,780
That's what makes it hard to stamp.

182
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:38,940
It isn't a moment.

183
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:40,960
It's the speed the sand falls.

184
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:43,400
So, did the sand run out here?

185
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:44,340
Not yet.

186
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:46,940
The blockade never won the war on its own.

187
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:52,240
But from now on, every German plan would
be a race against hunger as well as against

188
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:52,800
the enemy.

189
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:56,700
And that race would push Germany
toward the biggest gamble of the war.

190
00:09:56,980 --> 00:09:58,180
Keep a turnip in mind.

191
00:09:58,500 --> 00:09:59,200
It comes back.

192
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:03,460
By the end of 1915, the man
who had replaced Moltke,

193
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:07,640
General Erich von Falkenhayn, had
a problem with no obvious answer.

194
00:10:08,060 --> 00:10:13,020
Attacks on the Western Front gained almost
nothing and cost enormous numbers of men.

195
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:16,200
So he went looking for a
different kind of battle.

196
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:19,260
According to the memoirs
he wrote after the war,

197
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:23,540
his idea was that France could still
be beaten without a breakthrough,

198
00:10:24,380 --> 00:10:27,220
if the French army could
be made to lose enough men.

199
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:30,620
Pick a place the French
could not afford to give up,

200
00:10:30,620 --> 00:10:33,220
and let German heavy guns do the killing.

201
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:37,780
The place he picked was Verdun, a
fortress town on the river Meuse.

202
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:40,980
The attack began in February 1916.

203
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:45,640
It became the longest battle of the
war, lasting about three hundred days.

204
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:49,400
And the arithmetic never worked
the way the plan said it would.

205
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:55,212
One modern calculation puts French
casualties at around three hundred and

206
00:10:55,212 --> 00:11:00,148
seventy-seven thousand and German
casualties at around three hundred and

207
00:11:00,148 --> 00:11:01,220
thirty-seven thousand.

208
00:11:01,220 --> 00:11:04,780
That's roughly seventy thousand
men a month, for most of a year,

209
00:11:05,140 --> 00:11:06,820
on the hills north of one town.

210
00:11:06,820 --> 00:11:10,400
The army that was supposed
to bleed was the German army,

211
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:10,740
too.

212
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:14,520
And while Verdun went on, the
other side opened two more doors.

213
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:20,720
In June, the Russian general Aleksei
Brusilov attacked on a wide front in the east

214
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:25,620
and smashed the Austro-Hungarian armies,
inflicting losses they would never really

215
00:11:25,620 --> 00:11:26,320
recover from.

216
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:29,940
Germany had to send divisions
east to hold its ally up,

217
00:11:29,940 --> 00:11:33,620
and Romania was encouraged to
join the war on the Allied side.

218
00:11:33,620 --> 00:11:38,260
Then, on the 1st of July, the British
and French attacked on the Somme.

219
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:43,577
On its first day, the British army suffered
fifty-seven thousand four hundred and

220
00:11:43,577 --> 00:11:48,220
seventy casualties, including nineteen
thousand two hundred and forty killed.

221
00:11:49,280 --> 00:11:52,200
It's still the worst day in the
history of the British Army.

222
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:55,280
But the battle went on for a
hundred and forty-one days,

223
00:11:55,680 --> 00:11:57,940
and the Germans facing it bled as well.

224
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:02,680
In September, the first tanks in
history crawled into action there.

225
00:12:02,680 --> 00:12:05,440
By the end of August, Falkenhayn was gone.

226
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:09,500
Hindenburg and Ludendorff took
over the German war effort,

227
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:14,240
and before long, they were running it like
a military dictatorship in all but name.

228
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:17,660
They tried to squeeze more
out of Germany at home,

229
00:12:17,660 --> 00:12:22,260
with a new programme that made every man
from seventeen to sixty liable for war

230
00:12:22,260 --> 00:12:22,700
service.

231
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:25,240
It never came close to its targets.

232
00:12:25,980 --> 00:12:30,100
On the other side of the wire, Britain
had already introduced conscription in

233
00:12:30,100 --> 00:12:30,560
January.

234
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:34,060
The Allies were not going
to run out of men first.

235
00:12:34,600 --> 00:12:36,080
Sir, a million casualties.

236
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:37,280
That has to be it.

237
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:38,400
Terrible, Porter.

238
00:12:39,060 --> 00:12:42,340
But at the end of 1916,
Germany still holds Belgium,

239
00:12:42,740 --> 00:12:45,240
still holds northern France,
and Russia is cracking.

240
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:46,560
The sand's falling.

241
00:12:47,100 --> 00:12:48,080
It hasn't run out.

242
00:12:48,380 --> 00:12:50,000
So, did the sand run out here?

243
00:12:50,420 --> 00:12:50,960
Not yet.

244
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:55,440
1916 proved that nobody could
win quickly on the Western Front,

245
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:58,780
and that Germany could not
win by out-killing the French.

246
00:12:59,300 --> 00:13:01,480
It did not prove that Germany had to lose.

247
00:13:02,140 --> 00:13:04,180
Quick one before the next train.

248
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:07,700
If you're enjoying this, a
like really helps the channel.

249
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,620
And unlike a Western Front offensive,
it actually gets somewhere.

250
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:14,420
If Germany couldn't break
the blockade on land,

251
00:13:14,660 --> 00:13:16,420
there was one other place it could try.

252
00:13:16,820 --> 00:13:17,260
At sea.

253
00:13:17,720 --> 00:13:22,340
Germany's battle fleet, the High Seas Fleet,
had been built for exactly this kind of

254
00:13:22,340 --> 00:13:24,460
war, and then spent most of it in port.

255
00:13:25,560 --> 00:13:27,880
It was smaller than the
Royal Navy's Grand Fleet,

256
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:29,540
and its admirals knew it.

257
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:33,040
So the new German commander,
Admiral Reinhard Scheer,

258
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:34,260
came up with a trap.

259
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:38,680
Lure out part of the British fleet,
destroy it with the whole German fleet,

260
00:13:39,080 --> 00:13:40,100
and even the odds.

261
00:13:40,740 --> 00:13:43,260
The British had been reading
German naval signals,

262
00:13:43,620 --> 00:13:45,780
and they knew a big operation was coming.

263
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:52,600
On the 30th of May, 1916, the whole Grand
Fleet under Admiral Sir John Jellicoe put

264
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:53,080
to sea.

265
00:13:53,540 --> 00:13:56,240
The next afternoon, off the
coast of Jutland in Denmark,

266
00:13:56,620 --> 00:13:57,620
the two fleets met.

267
00:13:58,460 --> 00:14:01,960
It was the only full-scale clash
of battleships in the whole war.

268
00:14:02,580 --> 00:14:04,320
On paper, the Germans won it.

269
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:09,260
Their ninety-nine ships sank about a
hundred and fifteen thousand tons of British

270
00:14:09,260 --> 00:14:09,880
warships.

271
00:14:10,540 --> 00:14:12,680
The British, with a hundred
and fifty-one ships,

272
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:16,060
sank about sixty-two
thousand tons of German ones.

273
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:19,760
More than twice as many
British sailors died as German.

274
00:14:20,460 --> 00:14:23,020
In Germany, schoolchildren
were given a holiday,

275
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:26,640
and the Kaiser announced a
new chapter in world history.

276
00:14:27,100 --> 00:14:28,320
So Germany won, sir?

277
00:14:29,220 --> 00:14:30,580
Germany won the afternoon.

278
00:14:31,220 --> 00:14:33,760
Check who was still blocking
the door the next morning.

279
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:36,760
Here's the paperwork, on the German side.

280
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:41,960
Admiral Scheer quickly understood that more
battles at that rate would wear out his

281
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:44,500
fleet long before they
wore out the British one.

282
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:48,940
In August, another sortie was
nearly caught by the Grand Fleet.

283
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:53,700
After that, the High Seas Fleet stopped
coming out into the North Sea to look for

284
00:14:53,700 --> 00:14:54,020
battle.

285
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:57,420
It turned its attention to
the Baltic for most of 1917.

286
00:14:58,140 --> 00:15:00,560
The blockade stayed exactly where it was.

287
00:15:01,660 --> 00:15:04,260
Historians still argue
about who won Jutland.

288
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:06,900
There's much less argument
about what it changed.

289
00:15:07,560 --> 00:15:12,620
By the end of 1916, the German navy had
accepted that its battleships could not

290
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:13,660
break the blockade.

291
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:16,520
That left it with only
one weapon that could.

292
00:15:17,220 --> 00:15:17,760
The submarine.

293
00:15:18,340 --> 00:15:19,840
So, did the sand run out here?

294
00:15:20,540 --> 00:15:21,020
Not yet.

295
00:15:21,380 --> 00:15:25,760
But Jutland closed one road, and left
Germany staring down the only one still open.

296
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:29,960
Germany had tried submarine warfare
against merchant ships before.

297
00:15:30,520 --> 00:15:35,360
In February 1915, it declared the
waters around Britain a war zone.

298
00:15:35,780 --> 00:15:40,760
In May that year, a U-boat torpedoed the
British liner Lusitania off the coast of

299
00:15:40,760 --> 00:15:41,060
Ireland.

300
00:15:41,060 --> 00:15:46,060
Of nearly two thousand people on board,
fewer than eight hundred survived.

301
00:15:46,900 --> 00:15:49,440
About a hundred and twenty-eight
of the dead were Americans.

302
00:15:50,260 --> 00:15:53,700
You'll often hear that the Lusitania
brought America into the war.

303
00:15:53,980 --> 00:15:54,660
It didn't.

304
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:55,920
Not then.

305
00:15:56,400 --> 00:16:01,480
America protested, threatened, and stayed
out, and Woodrow Wilson won re-election in

306
00:16:01,480 --> 00:16:05,220
1916 on the slogan that he had
kept the country out of war.

307
00:16:05,660 --> 00:16:08,620
America didn't declare war
for almost two more years.

308
00:16:09,120 --> 00:16:11,320
The Lusitania made Americans angry.

309
00:16:11,740 --> 00:16:13,240
Something else made them fight.

310
00:16:13,820 --> 00:16:18,160
In April 1916, Germany stopped sinking
merchant ships without warning.

311
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:22,000
But by the end of that year,
after Verdun, the Somme,

312
00:16:22,280 --> 00:16:26,320
Jutland and the Turnip Winter, the
German Admiralty had a new plan,

313
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:28,180
and a deadline to go with it.

314
00:16:28,640 --> 00:16:32,240
In December 1916, the head
of the Admiralty staff,

315
00:16:32,580 --> 00:16:36,800
Admiral Henning von Holtzendorff, sent
a memorandum that reached the Kaiser.

316
00:16:37,380 --> 00:16:41,020
The odd thing is that Holtzendorff
had once been a sceptic.

317
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:45,860
Before the war, he'd even been pushed into
retirement for opposing the naval race

318
00:16:45,860 --> 00:16:46,300
with Britain.

319
00:16:46,980 --> 00:16:49,800
Now he was the submarine's
loudest champion.

320
00:16:50,280 --> 00:16:51,320
His argument was simple.

321
00:16:51,800 --> 00:16:54,560
Sink British shipping without
warning and without limits,

322
00:16:55,320 --> 00:16:58,500
and Britain would be forced to
ask for peace within six months.

323
00:16:59,080 --> 00:17:02,260
Within six months, England will
be forced to sue for peace.

324
00:17:02,260 --> 00:17:05,280
The risk of American
intervention can be taken.

325
00:17:05,800 --> 00:17:06,740
The generals agreed.

326
00:17:07,380 --> 00:17:11,760
They knew perfectly well that unrestricted
submarine warfare meant war with the

327
00:17:11,760 --> 00:17:12,520
United States.

328
00:17:13,060 --> 00:17:16,600
They simply calculated that America
would be too slow to matter.

329
00:17:16,860 --> 00:17:19,100
And it was not a crazy calculation.

330
00:17:19,740 --> 00:17:25,420
In 1916, the entire United States Army
had only a little over a hundred thousand

331
00:17:25,420 --> 00:17:26,260
regular soldiers.

332
00:17:26,260 --> 00:17:29,100
On the 9th of January, 1917,

333
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,960
at a conference at the
castle of Pless, in Silesia,

334
00:17:33,140 --> 00:17:34,200
the Kaiser approved it.

335
00:17:34,660 --> 00:17:39,040
From the 1st of February, German submarines
would sink ships around Britain without

336
00:17:39,040 --> 00:17:40,960
warning, neutral or not.

337
00:17:41,560 --> 00:17:42,360
New timetable.

338
00:17:42,820 --> 00:17:43,760
Six months to London.

339
00:17:44,120 --> 00:17:45,520
Put the glass on the table, Porter.

340
00:17:46,060 --> 00:17:48,800
A week after Pless, the
German Foreign Secretary,

341
00:17:49,140 --> 00:17:53,100
Arthur Zimmermann, sent a coded
telegram to his ambassador in Mexico.

342
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:57,040
If America entered the war, Mexico
was to be offered an alliance,

343
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:00,760
money, and the return of
Texas, New Mexico and Arizona.

344
00:18:01,460 --> 00:18:04,720
The telegram even explained
why Germany was so confident.

345
00:18:05,620 --> 00:18:10,440
The employment of ruthless submarine warfare
now promises to compel England to make

346
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:11,740
peace in a few months.

347
00:18:12,400 --> 00:18:15,460
British intelligence
intercepted it and decoded it,

348
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:17,460
and it was made public in America.

349
00:18:18,160 --> 00:18:21,580
Americans were furious, and
the fury only grew when,

350
00:18:21,760 --> 00:18:25,780
on the 3rd of March, Zimmermann himself
publicly admitted it was genuine.

351
00:18:26,340 --> 00:18:30,740
Mexico's own generals, for the record,
looked at the idea of invading the United

352
00:18:30,740 --> 00:18:33,060
States and concluded it was hopeless.

353
00:18:33,700 --> 00:18:38,520
On the 2nd of April, Woodrow Wilson went
before Congress and asked for a declaration

354
00:18:38,520 --> 00:18:39,240
of war.

355
00:18:39,680 --> 00:18:41,900
The world must be made safe for democracy.

356
00:18:42,480 --> 00:18:47,360
On the 6th of April, 1917, the United
States declared war on Germany.

357
00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:50,960
For a while, it looked as though
Holtzendorff might be right.

358
00:18:50,960 --> 00:18:57,120
In 1917, German submarines sank more
than six million tons of Allied shipping.

359
00:18:57,800 --> 00:19:02,000
But the British began sailing their
merchant ships in escorted convoys,

360
00:19:02,160 --> 00:19:03,200
and the sinkings fell.

361
00:19:03,660 --> 00:19:05,920
Britain did not ask for
peace in six months.

362
00:19:06,260 --> 00:19:07,340
It didn't ask at all.

363
00:19:07,720 --> 00:19:09,340
Sir, so this is the one?

364
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:10,080
Maybe.

365
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:14,360
I'm putting this stamp in my waistcoat
pocket until the last train's in.

366
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:19,200
Because there's one moment still to come
that turns the whole glass back over.

367
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:24,260
While America was declaring war in the
west, the eastern front was falling apart.

368
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:28,100
In 1917, revolution
swept the Russian Empire.

369
00:19:28,820 --> 00:19:31,200
The German high command
even helped it along.

370
00:19:31,660 --> 00:19:35,960
Ludendorff agreed to let a group of
Russian revolutionaries living in exile in

371
00:19:35,960 --> 00:19:40,600
Switzerland, Lenin's Bolsheviks,
travel home by train across Germany.

372
00:19:40,940 --> 00:19:43,420
In November, the Bolsheviks seized power.

373
00:19:43,860 --> 00:19:46,660
Lenin's first act was a
decree calling for peace.

374
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:50,420
By December, the guns had
stopped on the eastern front.

375
00:19:50,940 --> 00:19:57,000
The peace talks dragged, so in February
1918 the Germans simply attacked again and

376
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,420
marched deep into the Baltic
lands, Belarus and Ukraine.

377
00:20:01,100 --> 00:20:06,560
On the 3rd of March, 1918, the Bolsheviks
signed the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk.

378
00:20:06,560 --> 00:20:10,580
Russia gave up Poland, the Baltic
provinces, Belarus and Ukraine.

379
00:20:10,580 --> 00:20:15,700
Measured against the old Russian Empire,
the lost lands held about a third of its

380
00:20:15,700 --> 00:20:20,740
population, more than half of its industrial
land and almost nine-tenths of its

381
00:20:20,740 --> 00:20:21,880
coalfields.

382
00:20:21,880 --> 00:20:26,120
A later agreement that summer added
six billion marks in reparations,

383
00:20:26,120 --> 00:20:27,340
payable to Germany.

384
00:20:27,760 --> 00:20:31,520
For Germany, this was everything
it had dreamed of in 1914,

385
00:20:31,520 --> 00:20:33,460
three and a half years late.

386
00:20:33,900 --> 00:20:35,300
The two-front war was over.

387
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:40,800
The grain fields of Ukraine were open,
and Germany's ally Austria-Hungary,

388
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:44,980
whose capital Vienna was close to
starvation, wanted that grain desperately.

389
00:20:45,640 --> 00:20:46,740
Remember the turnip.

390
00:20:47,120 --> 00:20:48,440
This was the turnip's answer.

391
00:20:48,920 --> 00:20:52,560
And around fifty German divisions
were suddenly free to go west.

392
00:20:53,020 --> 00:20:54,440
Porter, give me a hand.

393
00:20:54,720 --> 00:20:55,980
We're turning the glass over.

394
00:20:56,440 --> 00:20:57,700
Sir, which way?

395
00:20:58,180 --> 00:20:59,000
Germany's way.

396
00:20:59,220 --> 00:21:00,760
For the first time in three years.

397
00:21:01,240 --> 00:21:03,140
So, did the sand run out here?

398
00:21:03,500 --> 00:21:03,840
No.

399
00:21:04,260 --> 00:21:05,660
Here, it ran back.

400
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:10,860
For the first time since 1914, Germany
had more divisions in the west than the

401
00:21:10,860 --> 00:21:11,460
Allies did.

402
00:21:11,760 --> 00:21:15,680
But this glass was smaller than the last
one, and everyone in the German high

403
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:17,020
command knew how small.

404
00:21:17,540 --> 00:21:18,760
The Americans were coming.

405
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:23,900
Ludendorff had decided on a great offensive
in the west back in November 1917,

406
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:26,120
before Russia had even signed.

407
00:21:26,640 --> 00:21:30,060
He was not sure the Americans
would ever arrive in strength.

408
00:21:30,400 --> 00:21:32,680
He was sure he could not wait to find out.

409
00:21:32,680 --> 00:21:36,960
On the 21st of March,
1918, the first blow fell,

410
00:21:36,960 --> 00:21:39,240
near the old Somme battlefields.

411
00:21:39,240 --> 00:21:41,380
The Germans had found a new way to attack.

412
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:44,760
Instead of long bombardments
and waves of infantry,

413
00:21:45,220 --> 00:21:49,660
small groups of specially trained stormtroopers
slipped through weak points in the

414
00:21:49,660 --> 00:21:54,660
line, bypassed strong points, and went
for the headquarters and the guns behind.

415
00:21:55,660 --> 00:21:56,080
It worked.

416
00:21:56,080 --> 00:22:01,520
The first and biggest blow, codenamed
Michael, was meant to break through the

417
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:06,160
British line, swing north and roll the
British army back toward the Channel.

418
00:22:06,820 --> 00:22:10,920
When Michael slowed, Ludendorff
launched another offensive in Flanders,

419
00:22:11,260 --> 00:22:13,660
then another against the
French on the river Aisne,

420
00:22:13,920 --> 00:22:14,700
then another.

421
00:22:15,240 --> 00:22:17,560
Five in all, between March and July.

422
00:22:18,180 --> 00:22:22,740
Together, they made the deepest advances
on the Western Front since 1914.

423
00:22:23,460 --> 00:22:27,860
By the 3rd of June, the Germans were
about fifty-six kilometres from Paris.

424
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:28,880
And yet.

425
00:22:29,680 --> 00:22:32,060
The offensive had no clear strategic aim.

426
00:22:32,620 --> 00:22:36,820
Its targets changed from week to week,
chosen because they looked tactically

427
00:22:36,820 --> 00:22:40,000
promising rather than because
taking them would end the war.

428
00:22:40,660 --> 00:22:45,660
Capturing a really decisive objective,
like the Channel ports or the great railway

429
00:22:45,660 --> 00:22:49,560
junction at Amiens, would have
happened by luck rather than design.

430
00:22:49,560 --> 00:22:53,160
The stormtroopers were the
best men in every division,

431
00:22:53,660 --> 00:22:55,540
and they were the ones who died first.

432
00:22:56,060 --> 00:22:58,140
Supplies couldn't keep
up with the advance.

433
00:22:58,520 --> 00:23:03,796
In one phase alone, up to early June,
each side lost about a hundred and thirty

434
00:23:03,796 --> 00:23:04,380
thousand men.

435
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:06,840
Germany could not afford that trade.

436
00:23:07,860 --> 00:23:11,960
Its own high command reckoned the army
needed two hundred thousand replacements a

437
00:23:11,960 --> 00:23:12,260
month.

438
00:23:13,340 --> 00:23:16,020
Recovered wounded could supply
seventy or eighty thousand.

439
00:23:16,020 --> 00:23:21,750
A whole year's class of eighteen-year-olds
came to about three hundred thousand.

440
00:23:21,750 --> 00:23:26,160
Ludendorff admitted in private that Germany
could no longer win a war of attrition.

441
00:23:26,880 --> 00:23:29,460
He still wouldn't give up
the territory it had taken,

442
00:23:29,940 --> 00:23:31,440
in the west or the east.

443
00:23:32,060 --> 00:23:34,600
Now remember Admiral
Holtzendorff's six months.

444
00:23:35,200 --> 00:23:41,140
In June 1917, six months after Pless, only
fourteen thousand American soldiers had

445
00:23:41,140 --> 00:23:41,860
reached France.

446
00:23:41,860 --> 00:23:45,320
By May 1918, there were
more than a million.

447
00:23:45,960 --> 00:23:49,320
By June, they were arriving at
the rate of ten thousand a day.

448
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:55,340
In 1916, the whole American army had been
a little over a hundred thousand men.

449
00:23:55,960 --> 00:24:00,220
Before the war was over, two million
American soldiers would cross the Atlantic.

450
00:24:00,900 --> 00:24:05,100
The submarines hadn't starved Britain,
and they hadn't stopped the troopships

451
00:24:05,100 --> 00:24:05,460
either.

452
00:24:05,460 --> 00:24:10,940
By July, Germany's lead on the Western
Front had shrunk to two hundred and seven

453
00:24:10,940 --> 00:24:15,760
divisions against two hundred and
three, and it was shrinking every day.

454
00:24:16,180 --> 00:24:17,640
Sir, ten thousand a day.

455
00:24:17,920 --> 00:24:19,000
That's a train every hour.

456
00:24:19,500 --> 00:24:20,420
More than one, Porter.

457
00:24:20,800 --> 00:24:21,900
And all of them on time.

458
00:24:22,600 --> 00:24:27,120
On the 15th of July, 1918, the
Germans attacked one last time,

459
00:24:27,120 --> 00:24:29,880
on both sides of Reims,
near the river Marne.

460
00:24:30,120 --> 00:24:30,860
It went nowhere.

461
00:24:30,860 --> 00:24:34,740
On the 18th of July, the new
Allied supreme commander,

462
00:24:35,200 --> 00:24:37,760
the French general
Ferdinand Foch, hit back.

463
00:24:38,560 --> 00:24:43,060
Two dozen French divisions, with American
divisions fighting alongside them and

464
00:24:43,060 --> 00:24:46,920
several hundred small Renault
tanks, broke into the German flank.

465
00:24:47,620 --> 00:24:49,160
The Germans had to retreat.

466
00:24:49,680 --> 00:24:52,460
It was the last German offensive
of the war in the west.

467
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:55,960
Almost four years after the
first battle of the Marne,

468
00:24:56,240 --> 00:24:58,020
the second one went the same way.

469
00:24:58,380 --> 00:25:00,000
So, did the sand run out here?

470
00:25:00,320 --> 00:25:00,680
Yes.

471
00:25:01,240 --> 00:25:05,320
On the 18th of July, 1918,
Germany's last window closed.

472
00:25:05,560 --> 00:25:07,260
It never attacked in the west again.

473
00:25:07,660 --> 00:25:10,960
From here on, the only question
was how long it would take,

474
00:25:11,480 --> 00:25:15,460
and how many more men would die before
the German high command admitted it.

475
00:25:15,900 --> 00:25:17,080
They didn't have to wait long.

476
00:25:17,900 --> 00:25:22,980
On the 8th of August, 1918, near Amiens,
the British Fourth Army attacked,

477
00:25:22,980 --> 00:25:28,320
with the Australian Corps under General
John Monash and the Canadian Corps under

478
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:32,140
General Arthur Currie in the
lead, and a French army alongside.

479
00:25:32,940 --> 00:25:36,900
The plan was built largely by
Monash, using the same mix of tanks,

480
00:25:37,400 --> 00:25:42,080
aircraft and carefully timed artillery
he had tried out five weeks earlier in a

481
00:25:42,080 --> 00:25:43,420
smaller battle at Hamel.

482
00:25:43,740 --> 00:25:47,060
This time there was no long
bombardment to warn the Germans.

483
00:25:47,900 --> 00:25:52,340
The guns opened up at the very moment
the infantry and around six hundred tanks

484
00:25:52,340 --> 00:25:53,120
moved forward.

485
00:25:53,820 --> 00:25:57,360
On the first day, the Allies
advanced about eleven kilometres,

486
00:25:57,900 --> 00:26:00,620
one of the biggest single-day
advances of the war.

487
00:26:01,080 --> 00:26:03,700
Whole groups of German
soldiers simply gave up.

488
00:26:04,280 --> 00:26:06,060
Ludendorff later gave the day a name.

489
00:26:06,580 --> 00:26:08,280
The black day of the German Army.

490
00:26:08,720 --> 00:26:10,920
It wasn't the ground
lost that frightened him.

491
00:26:11,360 --> 00:26:12,540
It was the surrenders.

492
00:26:13,240 --> 00:26:15,920
The German army had stopped
believing it could win.

493
00:26:16,300 --> 00:26:20,840
Over the following weeks, the Allies pushed
the Germans back to the Hindenburg Line,

494
00:26:21,260 --> 00:26:24,300
the great fortified line they
had started from in the spring.

495
00:26:25,240 --> 00:26:30,020
On the 29th of September, the British
broke through it at the St Quentin Canal.

496
00:26:30,320 --> 00:26:34,280
And that was the same day Hindenburg
and Ludendorff went to the Kaiser,

497
00:26:34,680 --> 00:26:37,120
told him the front might
not hold for two hours,

498
00:26:37,680 --> 00:26:39,480
and asked for an armistice.

499
00:26:40,080 --> 00:26:41,660
That's the moment we started with.

500
00:26:42,300 --> 00:26:43,760
Now you know how they got there.

501
00:26:44,220 --> 00:26:49,000
In early October, Germany's government asked
President Wilson for peace talks on the

502
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:50,640
basis of his Fourteen Points.

503
00:26:51,260 --> 00:26:52,680
Ludendorff was forced out.

504
00:26:53,340 --> 00:26:58,280
Sailors mutinied, revolution spread,
and on the 9th of November the Kaiser's

505
00:26:58,280 --> 00:26:59,500
abdication was announced.

506
00:27:00,160 --> 00:27:04,480
Two days later, at five in the morning,
in a railway carriage in the forest of

507
00:27:04,480 --> 00:27:06,780
Compiègne, the armistice was signed.

508
00:27:07,400 --> 00:27:09,430
It came into force at eleven o'clock.

509
00:27:09,430 --> 00:27:14,320
Two thousand seven hundred and thirty-eight
men died on that last morning of the

510
00:27:14,320 --> 00:27:14,580
war.

511
00:27:15,160 --> 00:27:17,820
Which brings us to the
last myth on this line.

512
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:23,160
Within months of the armistice, some of
Germany's own officers were telling a very

513
00:27:23,160 --> 00:27:23,920
different story.

514
00:27:24,740 --> 00:27:28,020
One of Ludendorff's closest
advisers, Colonel Max Bauer,

515
00:27:28,580 --> 00:27:32,080
published a book in the spring
of 1919 with a clear answer.

516
00:27:32,540 --> 00:27:36,580
The war was lost only and exclusively
through the failure of the homeland.

517
00:27:37,120 --> 00:27:39,840
The army, so the story
went, had never been beaten.

518
00:27:40,400 --> 00:27:43,540
It had been stabbed in the
back by strikers, socialists,

519
00:27:43,720 --> 00:27:45,420
republican politicians and Jews.

520
00:27:46,280 --> 00:27:48,920
Ludendorff became one of
its loudest promoters.

521
00:27:49,380 --> 00:27:52,160
Later, the Nazis made it part
of their official history.

522
00:27:52,540 --> 00:27:54,000
Sir, is that one true?

523
00:27:54,480 --> 00:27:55,880
Look at the timetable, Porter.

524
00:27:56,340 --> 00:27:58,080
Who asked for the armistice first?

525
00:27:58,640 --> 00:27:59,540
Here's the paperwork.

526
00:28:00,120 --> 00:28:05,260
On the 29th of September, it was the army's
own commanders who told the Kaiser the

527
00:28:05,260 --> 00:28:07,260
front could collapse within hours.

528
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:11,080
They asked for the armistice
before the revolution began.

529
00:28:11,580 --> 00:28:15,820
To be fair to the myth's strongest version,
the German army had not been destroyed

530
00:28:15,820 --> 00:28:16,660
in November.

531
00:28:17,300 --> 00:28:22,120
When the Allies discussed terms, Foch
himself agreed that the Germans could still

532
00:28:22,120 --> 00:28:26,240
fall back to a new line, and that
the Allies could not stop them.

533
00:28:26,520 --> 00:28:29,960
But an army that is retreating
every week, losing allies,

534
00:28:30,440 --> 00:28:33,540
and surrendering in thousands
is not an undefeated army.

535
00:28:34,040 --> 00:28:38,720
That's why Foch's terms were so hard:
German troops out of everything west of the

536
00:28:38,720 --> 00:28:42,540
Rhine, Allied troops on the Rhine,
and the handover of warships,

537
00:28:42,820 --> 00:28:45,080
aircraft and huge quantities of weapons.

538
00:28:45,700 --> 00:28:49,520
Historians accept that hunger and
unrest at home played their part.

539
00:28:49,820 --> 00:28:50,680
They reject the myth.

540
00:28:51,180 --> 00:28:53,140
It wasn't a fact about 1918.

541
00:28:53,780 --> 00:28:55,960
It was a weapon for the 1920s.

542
00:28:55,960 --> 00:28:58,280
So, let's read the timetable back.

543
00:28:58,760 --> 00:28:59,920
Marne, 1914.

544
00:29:00,680 --> 00:29:01,600
The short war, finished.

545
00:29:02,100 --> 00:29:02,460
Not yet.

546
00:29:02,620 --> 00:29:03,100
Blockade.

547
00:29:03,220 --> 00:29:04,140
The speed of the sand.

548
00:29:04,440 --> 00:29:04,880
Not yet.

549
00:29:05,180 --> 00:29:06,380
Verdun and the Somme.

550
00:29:06,380 --> 00:29:06,820
Not yet.

551
00:29:07,120 --> 00:29:07,400
Jutland.

552
00:29:07,660 --> 00:29:09,240
Not yet, but one road closed.

553
00:29:09,240 --> 00:29:10,840
Brest-Litovsk.

554
00:29:10,840 --> 00:29:11,760
Glass turned back over.

555
00:29:11,920 --> 00:29:13,320
The 18th of July, 1918.

556
00:29:13,880 --> 00:29:14,440
Sand out.

557
00:29:14,820 --> 00:29:15,240
And Amiens.

558
00:29:15,660 --> 00:29:17,000
The day the army saw it.

559
00:29:17,420 --> 00:29:20,060
And one stamp is still in
the stationmaster's pocket.

560
00:29:20,440 --> 00:29:20,660
Pless.

561
00:29:21,340 --> 00:29:22,240
Here's our verdict.

562
00:29:22,240 --> 00:29:25,440
The Marne decided that
Germany could not win fast.

563
00:29:25,760 --> 00:29:28,160
Pless decided that it
could not win slowly.

564
00:29:28,780 --> 00:29:30,560
On the 9th of January, 1917,

565
00:29:31,400 --> 00:29:34,780
Germany's leaders chose to add the
United States to their enemies,

566
00:29:34,780 --> 00:29:38,440
in exchange for a promise that
Britain would starve in six months.

567
00:29:39,120 --> 00:29:40,320
Britain didn't starve.

568
00:29:40,780 --> 00:29:41,680
America came.

569
00:29:42,140 --> 00:29:46,780
From that day on, every German chance,
including the one Russia's collapse handed

570
00:29:46,780 --> 00:29:50,300
them in 1918, was a race
against American ships.

571
00:29:50,300 --> 00:29:53,180
Brest-Litovsk turned the glass over.

572
00:29:53,720 --> 00:29:56,920
Pless had already decided
how much sand was left in it.

573
00:29:57,160 --> 00:30:01,520
And Moltke's sentence from 1914,
the one nobody can prove he said?

574
00:30:02,300 --> 00:30:06,620
"Your Majesty, we have lost the
war" was wrong in September 1914.

575
00:30:07,160 --> 00:30:09,520
Germany had more than two
years of chances left.

576
00:30:10,100 --> 00:30:13,080
By the evening of the
9th of January, 1917,

577
00:30:13,880 --> 00:30:16,520
somebody in that castle at
Pless could have said it,

578
00:30:16,520 --> 00:30:17,380
and been right.

579
00:30:17,380 --> 00:30:21,900
Now, to be fair, historians don't
all put the stamp in the same place.

580
00:30:22,720 --> 00:30:28,020
Many still point to the Marne, the battle
one historian called the most important of

581
00:30:28,020 --> 00:30:31,400
the twentieth century, because
once the quick victory failed,

582
00:30:31,860 --> 00:30:34,960
Germany was trapped in the
long war it could not afford.

583
00:30:35,480 --> 00:30:40,160
Others put the weight on the blockade,
which squeezed Germany every single day of

584
00:30:40,160 --> 00:30:40,560
the war.

585
00:30:40,900 --> 00:30:43,920
And others say the war was
decided where it ended,

586
00:30:44,260 --> 00:30:47,700
by the Allied armies in the
summer and autumn of 1918,

587
00:30:48,320 --> 00:30:51,860
who learned to beat the German
army in the field and finally did.

588
00:30:52,540 --> 00:30:56,020
The strongest case against our
stamp is the spring of 1918.

589
00:30:56,920 --> 00:31:00,720
If Germany could come within fifty-six
kilometres of Paris after Pless,

590
00:31:01,240 --> 00:31:03,260
was the war really lost at Pless?

591
00:31:03,860 --> 00:31:04,880
It's a fair question.

592
00:31:05,400 --> 00:31:07,660
But look at what the
offensives were aiming at.

593
00:31:08,020 --> 00:31:12,240
They never had a clear goal, and the one
target that might have split the Allies,

594
00:31:12,240 --> 00:31:17,640
the rail junction at Amiens, was exactly
where the Allies piled up their reserves.

595
00:31:18,280 --> 00:31:20,760
Taking it would have
needed luck, not a plan.

596
00:31:21,140 --> 00:31:24,760
And by the summer, every week the
Germans spent looking for that luck,

597
00:31:24,760 --> 00:31:28,440
as many as seventy thousand
more Americans came ashore.

598
00:31:29,120 --> 00:31:32,820
All of these historians are
describing parts of the same clock.

599
00:31:33,340 --> 00:31:36,260
The price of that clock
was almost beyond counting.

600
00:31:36,260 --> 00:31:41,300
Somewhere between nine and eleven million
soldiers died in the First World War.

601
00:31:41,700 --> 00:31:46,400
With civilians, the total runs to something
like fifteen to twenty-two million.

602
00:31:46,900 --> 00:31:48,640
Millions more came home wounded.

603
00:31:49,200 --> 00:31:50,840
That's our reading of the hourglass.

604
00:31:51,600 --> 00:31:52,100
What's yours?

605
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:56,540
Would you stamp the Marne, Pless,
or the black day at Amiens?

606
00:31:56,540 --> 00:31:57,460
Tell us in the comments.

607
00:31:58,080 --> 00:32:00,700
And if you want more wars told
from both sides of the wire,

608
00:32:01,100 --> 00:32:01,460
subscribe.

609
00:32:01,460 --> 00:32:06,140
It's the only timetable in this whole
story that actually runs on time.

610
00:32:06,900 --> 00:32:10,860
The war that began with railway
timetables ended in a railway carriage,

611
00:32:10,860 --> 00:32:13,820
in a forest north of Paris,
at five in the morning.

612
00:32:14,480 --> 00:32:16,880
Germany had spent four
years racing the clock.

613
00:32:17,440 --> 00:32:19,780
In the end, it never
caught a single train.

614
00:32:20,320 --> 00:32:21,100
Last train in.

615
00:32:21,100 --> 00:32:21,940
Stamp the log.
